          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does asthma guidance describe as the stepwise approach, and what changed about reliever choice?",
          "answer": "Asthma guidance is organised in **steps**. The step is chosen from current symptom control and exacerbation history, and it is reviewed over time rather than fixed. The passages I retrieved do not describe what each step contains, so I cannot set out the full ladder.\n\n**The main change:** Treating asthma with a short-acting bronchodilator (SABA) alone is no longer considered appropriate at any severity. Every adult and adolescent with asthma should receive inhaled-corticosteroid-containing therapy. Reliever-only treatment leaves the underlying inflammation untreated and is linked to severe exacerbations, even in people with infrequent symptoms. Heavy reliever use, such as several canisters a year, is itself a marker of risk. (Source: *Asthma: confirming the diagnosis and the stepwise approach to controller therapy*.)\n\n**Where authorities differ:** The preferred reliever in mild asthma is a genuine disagreement.\n- Global strategy guidance describes an inhaled corticosteroid plus formoterol combination as the preferred reliever across steps.\n- Some national panel guidance says a short-acting bronchodilator reliever alongside a regular controller remains appropriate in mild disease.\n\nThe passages do not say which specific documents hold these positions. They also do not give the evidence strength behind either view, so I cannot say how firm the evidence is for the combination reliever. I have not picked a side.\n\n**Review:** Review one to three months after starting or changing treatment, then every three to twelve months. Review within one week after an exacerbation.\n\n**Other points from the retrieved material:**\n- Control and severity are different. Control can change within weeks. Severity is judged once treatment is established. Poor control is often a technique or adherence problem, so a step-up may treat the wrong problem.\n- Rising use of a relief inhaler suggests the asthma is not well controlled.\n- Asthma care also includes checking inhaler technique, a written action plan, and treating contributing conditions.\n\nIf you or someone you know has breathlessness at rest, cannot speak in full sentences, has blue lips or fingers, or is drowsy or confused, call emergency services now. A chest that has gone quiet during an attack is also a warning sign.\n\nThis is general information, not advice about your own treatment. Questions about which reliever or step fits a particular person are for their clinician.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
          "wall_ms": 9738.67945699999,
          "red_flag_matched": false,
